ighting between u.s led forces and the  taliban 14 years ago  his brother and one of his sons was  killed  he and his family have lived in this  camp for internally displaced people in  kabul ever since  he blames the united states and its  allies for the pain they've endured  in the past 20 years we've suffered so  much that at times we've hardly been  able to eat for days all afghans have  been through terrible hardships  bombardments fighting our houses  destroyed the war lasted so long because  we are afghans and we will fight for our  country  in central kabul today taliban fighters  guard former government buildings and  abandoned foreign embassies  the challenges the movement faces after  what it describes as victory in a fight  for national sovereignty are immense  it's been 20 years since the 9 11  attacks and the soon after u.s lead  invasion to hunt down osama bin laden  and remove the taliban from power  then followed what the taliban described  as two decades  of foreign occupation  the foreign forces have gone the taliban  are back in power  and the un says that afghanistan is  facing a humanitarian and economic  catastrophe  this is the old american embassy  once a hub of cia and military planning  and one of the most important centres of  u.s foreign policy in the world  now it's just an empty shell  hamid ghoul was 24 when u.s led  coalition forces invaded he is scared  about what the future holds  in the last 20 years my hopes changed  but now they've been left behind all  afghans thought we would get close to  the rest of the world when the americans  were here but now we have to wait and  see again what the future will bring  despite his doubts hamid says he wants  the world to give the taliban a chance  all my life i've experienced only war i  just want to sleep with ease i just want  to wake up with no fear i'm tired of  saying goodbye to my family as i leave  for work afraid i'll never see them  again  this is one of many graveyards on  hillsides surrounding kabul  here are buried some of the estimated 50  000 afghan civilians killed in the last  20 years  inzaghul says he also wants the  international community to accept the  taliban he wants education for his  children who are born into war  he wants to chance for them all to start  their lives again  and so so much is dependent on the  international community um accepting the  taliban and the taliban sticking to  their commitments the promises that  they've made as they say on human rights  uh protecting women um and on as i say  not  letting this country for example be used  as a as a base for terror attacks to be  um launched against other countries the  un is warning that if aid is not  released that aid currently being frozen  because the taliban because of the  taliban have retaken power the u.n  saying that if that aid is not unfrozen  potentially almost 100 percent of the  population of afghanistan could be in  poverty by around about this time next  year  so in terms of what everyday afghans are  thinking they're thinking well we're  very relieved thank god the war is over  but there are huge anxieties and  challenges ahead
 

